A||N||6||casto9||Questions for Fiero Experts!||||4||
Z||000000||casto9||07-20-2000||12:09 PM||||Ok, here's the lowdown. Please remember that I am not a mechanical person, but am trying to learn.<BR>1.) How do you bleed the clutch?  <P>2.) I need help with how to tune up my 87 gt.  Can't get to plugs in back.  Any info. on any part of a tune up will help.<P>3.) Whenever I go around a corner where i'm turning left just a little too fast, (not too fast for a normal fiero to handle), the car kind of shimmies to the side and the tires squeel easily. Kind of feels like the front end is grabbing but the back end is sliding. A friend told me that he thought the tie rod was broken or damaged.  <P>Thanks for all the help.  I love the fiero and am trying to learn everything I can about it.  Only 18 yrs. old, but already own two of them.  (4cyl 87, and 87 gt).  All my friends make fun of my cars from a mechanical standpoint, but admit they look good.  Just wish I could prove to them what a great machine a mint fiero is.  Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by casto9 (edited 07-21-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||07-20-2000||12:50 PM||||<BR>I'll chip in.<P>1) For clutch info go here: <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/archism.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/archism.htm</A>  - just one addition, on the clutch bleeding, while on step 10, after topping off the reservoir reattach the reservoir cap.<P>2) I change the back plugs, by removing the rear deck lid, it takes me just a few minutes to remove and reattach the lid. Another way some guys do it is to use a swivel joint extension to reach the plugs.<P>btw, welcome to the forum and continue working on the fiero(s) your friends will come around.<P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 07-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||07-20-2000||12:55 PM||||Welcome to the forum!<P>The first two technical questions you have, can be answered if you'll use the "search" function.  <P>One way to learn more about the Fiero, is to pick up a Haynes Fiero manual, if you don't already have one -available at many parts stores at a reasonable cost.  For the tune-up, read out any trouble codes stored in the ECM, check & gap the plugs, change air filter element if necessary, etc., check the compression & ignition timing.  It's not difficult at all.<P>Don't waste too much time trying to convince others what a great machine the Fiero is.  If they have any smarts, they'll figure it out.  Be patient. If they don't come around, who cares?  You only have to please yourself.<P>I knew one person that criticized Fieros up one side and down the other.  For years.  I would just smile and respond, "Fine."  Now he owns a Fiero.  Says he loves it and he "always <B>did</B> like these little cars."              ||||reg||14
Z||000003||Taijiguy||07-20-2000||01:06 PM||||I can make some suggestions regarding the rear end breaking loose-<P>It could be the result of a number of things, the first being the simplest: check your tire pressure. Generally tires are recommended to be at 32 psi, but for "spirited" driving, pump them up to 40-42 psi, if they're in decent shape, they won't pop.<P>Check the sway bar end links. If you're car is equipped with a sway bar, often the end links crap out. They are the ball joint type ends. Usually replacements can be purchased at a good hardware store, or Home Depot believe it or not. Keep the new ones well greased to preserve them. (If you don't have a sway bar- put one on!)<P>Check the shocks. If the rear shocks are shot, they won't be able to kep the tires on the ground if there's any irregularity in the road surface.<P>Have the alignment checked. Fieros can be four wheel aligned. if the rear end is out of alignment it will throw the rear end geometry off when one side lifts from the turn.<P>Maybe you're just outdriving the capabilities of your tires. 75 series tires suck for anything but land barges that are meant to feel like they're never moving. 60's are better, but still allow a lot of sidewall roll. 50's are pretty much ideal for road use providing enough sidewall flex to be palatible for daily driving and shock absorption, yet short enough to inhibit too much sidewall roll.<P>Sorry about the length- hope this provides some insight to your handling problem....<P>Taiji||||reg||4
Z||000004||terryk||07-20-2000||04:11 PM||||Stugood,<P>I was one of those people. I now own two.||||reg||1
Z||000005||RiceCooker||07-21-2000||07:29 PM||||Welcome to the forum casto9!<P>I've been here about six months and i'm still kinda in the same boat as you but i've learned so much from everyone here. As for the hard to reach sparkplugs, you don't need to take off the decklid. Just buy a flexhandle ratshit ($10) and you can do them in 10 mins each. You have to stand in the trunk and your hands might get scraped a bit, but oh well.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||07-22-2000||11:33 AM||||I got to where I could do all six plugs in a half hour before I shifted to a better brand (not gonna go there!!!).  <BR>For the forward plugs, I found that using a plug socket (most have a 3/4" sixpoint head), a 3/4 inch, 3/8" drive socket, a 1/4" female to 3/8" male adapter, and a 1/4" ratchet handle gives enough flexibilty, yet it's easy to move around in there.  <BR>Close the trunk lid about 1/3 of the way (moves the higes enough for your hands), stand in the trunk, and you can fit your hands around each side of the engine to reach the plugs and wires.  <BR>Taylor Spiro-pro wires have boots that are almost impossible to break, and you don't need the boot heat shields anymore.  Don't forget the anti-sieze on the threads.<BR> Accel cap and rotor kit doesn't seem to corrode as fast as the Delco version.<BR>Replace the pickup coil in the distributor if you're having starting problems or have a lot of miles.  Mine started giving me trouble at 160,000 miles. <BR>For bleeding the clutch, I use a Mity-vac.  Around $25 at Wally World.<BR>Check out the Fiero Online Service Guide if you haven't already.  <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/</A> <BR>||||reg||1
